            <ArticleTitle>Preemptive analgesia: problems with assessment of clinical significance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>475-82</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/978-1-60327-323-7_34</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The results of clinical studies on the value of preemptive analgesia are far from being unanimous. There are a number of potential problems related to preemptive analgesia that could lead to controversy regarding its clinical significance. The following potential problems are analyzed: (1) terminology, (2) approach to reveal the effect of preemptive analgesia, (3) verification of the direct pharmacological effect of a treatment, (4) partial preemptive effect in control, (5) intensity of noxious stimuli, (6) difference in a drug concentration between study groups during postoperative period, and (7) outcome measures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
